    "publishedAt": "2026-11-25",
    "summary": "Peter Thiel-backed startup has big plans to put compute at sea Some 250 million years ago, much of the Earth was covered by a superocean known as Panthalassa. Surrounding the supercontinent Pangea during the Paleozoic–Mesozoic transition, its waters coated some 70 percent of the planet’s surface, The original Panthalassa is long gone (its direct continuation is today’s Pacific Ocean), but the name lives on thanks to a startup that hopes to use energy from waves to power data centers. Sheldon-Coulson says the company can construct craft of various sizes, generating “anywhere from 200kW-800kW, but we think the economic optimum will probably be around 400kW. Though the company is not disclosing full details of its business model, or whether it intends to partner with a hyperscaler or neocloud, Sheldon-Coulson suggests it will have a “vertically-integrated” offering targeted at the burgeoning AI inferencing market.",
